WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.990
In 1885 a farmer in Vermont, Wilson Bentley, photographed a single snow crystal.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:11.570
Then he photographed another, and another: thousands more.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:14.310
He said he never found two the same.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:18.390
You've probably heard it too: no two snowflakes are alike.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:21.670
But is that actually true, or just a saying?

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:24.070
Are two snowflakes ever the same?

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:31.370
In this mission, you'll find out what decides a snow crystal's shape, and how scientists learned to read a snowflake.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:38.330
Then you'll put Bentley's claim to the test: how do you check a claim about every snowflake that has ever fallen?

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:42.610
Your mission: decide whether two snowflakes can ever be the same.
